      "text": "To solve this problem, we can set up a system of equations to represent the given information. Let's denote the cost of a pen by \\( p \\) and the cost of a pencil by \\( c \\). The problem gives us two equations:\n\n1. \\( 4p + 3c = 224 \\) (because 2.24 dollars is equal to 224 cents)\n2. \\( 2p + 5c = 154 \\) (because 1.54 dollars is equal to 154 cents)\n\nWe need to find the value of \\( c \\). First, let's eliminate one of the variables by manipulating these equations. We can do this by making the coefficients of \\( p \\) (the same) in both equations. To do this, we can multiply the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 4:\n\nFirst equation: \\( 4p + 6c = 448 \\)\nSecond equation: \\( 8p + 20c = 616 \\)\n\nNow, we subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\( p \\):\n\n\\[\n(8p + 20c) - (4p + 6c) = 616 - 448\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 14c = 168\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find a way to express \\( p \\) in terms of \\( c \\) or vice versa. From the first equation, we can express \\( p \\) as:\n\n\\[\np = \\frac{224 - 3c}{4}\n\\]\n\nWe substitute this expression for \\( p \\) into the equation \\( 4p + 14c = 168 \\):\n\n\\[\n4\\left(\\frac{224 - 3c}{4}\\right) + 14c = 168\n\\]\n\\[\n224 - 3c + 14c = 168\n\\]\n\\[\n224 + 11c = 168\n\\]\n\\[\n11c = 168 - 224\n\\]\n\\[\n11c = -56\n\\]\n\\[\nc = -\\frac{56}{11}\n\\]\n\\[\nc = \\frac{56}{11}\n\\]\n\\[\nc = 5.0909\n\\]\n\\[\nc = 5.0909 \\times 100\n\\]\n\\[\nc = 50.91\n\\]\n\nThus, the cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{5.09}\\) cents.",
